  {
   "id": "H-002",
   "severity": "High",
   "evidence_level": "analyzer_and_reasoning",
   "title": "Lender freezes repaid collateral by reassigning the owner of their USDC ATA",
   "component": "loan",
   "location": {
    "path": "src/instructions/escrow_instructions/loan.rs",
    "start": 590,
    "end": 597
   },
   "category": "ATA owner reassign",
   "category_key": "solana.ata_owner_reassign",
   "description": [
    {
     "p": "Repayment is credited only inside settle_read, which runs from settle or claim_default. Both paths require lender_loan_token to be the lender's canonical USDC ATA with associated_token::authority = lender. The lender controls that account and, under classic SPL Token, can move its owner with SetAuthority AccountOwner, after which the constraint rejects it on every call."
    },
    {
     "p": "The borrower cannot move the escrow out of Active alone: claims require Idle or Defaulted, sweep rejects Active, and the payout destination is fixed by derivation."
    }
   ],
   "impact": [
    {
     "p": "A lender can keep a fully repaid loan in Active and hold the borrower's whole collateral position locked for as long as they want, which can be used to extort the borrower. The lender gains no direct profit, since restoring the ATA and calling claim_default would credit the pending repayment first and revert, but the borrower's collateral is unavailable until the lender cooperates."
    }
   ],
   "recommendation": [
    {
     "p": "Credit repayments without depending on a lender-controlled account: let settle record repaid and move the escrow to Idle even when the lender payout account is invalid, keeping the lender's share in a program-owned account that the lender withdraws with a pull-based instruction to any token account it owns."
    }
   ],
